module gunbc.rung_drop_amendment

import std.types { String, NonEmptyStr, List, Bool }
import gunbc.rung_drop { RungDropStanding, Standing, Retired }

// A LATER RULING AMENDING A STANDING RUNG DROP -- THE ONE THING gunbc.rung_drop COULD NOT CARRY.
// A DESIGN section 4b(3) declaration is written once, at the moment the guarantee falls: previous
// rung, temporary rung, reason, population, restoration trigger. It has no field for what happens
// NEXT while the row still stands -- and the world does move under a standing drop. gunbc#11774
// discovered that the hard way: its author had a fact the ruling depended on (the carrier redesign
// had already landed hours earlier, so the ruling was pricing the gate and not only its billing),
// no field to put it in, and wrote it into docs/design-rung-drops.md -- a PROJECTION of
// gunbc.rung_drop. Two lanes then met the unexplained deletion in their regeneration diff
// (gunbc#11732 reverted it, gunbc#11786 was left holding nothing else and was closed), and the
// third regeneration, gunbc#11789, carried the deletion and the reasoning left the corpus. That is
// DESIGN section 6's manual application committed as source, and the repair is not to re-hand-edit
// the bytes: it is this carrier.
//
// WHY THIS CARRIES NO CLAIM ABOUT THE RESTORATION TRIGGER. The first draft of this type had an
// `AmendedTrigger = TriggerStandsWhole | TriggerSuperseded` field, so that the amendment could say
// what it did to the declaration below it. That is a SECOND AUTHORITY for a fact the drop already
// owns: whether the guarantee is still down is `RungDropStanding` on the row, and whether the
// trigger fired is `Retired { trigger_fired }` -- so an amendment asserting the trigger stands
// whole beside a Retired row would be two answers to one question, and DESIGN section 4b(3) is
// explicit that a drop is retired BY ITS TRIGGER AND BY NOTHING ELSE. The sentence is therefore
// DERIVED from the row's own standing at render time and is authored nowhere. An amendment states
// what happened in the world and what it bears on; it does not get a vote on the declaration.
//
// THE BOUNDARY THAT LEAVES, STATED BECAUSE THE TYPE CANNOT STATE IT. The derivation above is honest
// for one class of amendment and one only: a later ruling that bears on a standing drop WITHOUT
// MOVING ITS RESTORATION TRIGGER. A ruling that SUPERSEDES the trigger -- replacing the condition
// that retires the row -- has no representation here, and standing cannot stand in for it: such a
// row is still `Standing`, so `standing_sentence` would close it with "the restoration trigger above
// stands whole", which would be FALSE. That is the one thing this carrier can say wrongly.
//
// It is left unmodelled rather than built because NOTHING IN THE CORPUS SUPERSEDES A TRIGGER TODAY,
// and an arm with no instance is a constructor that constructs nothing -- the decoration DESIGN
// section 4b names at the top rung, permanently green and worse than absent because it would be
// cited as coverage. What arrives with the first real superseding ruling is an authored arm carrying
// THE SUPERSEDING TRIGGER ITSELF (nobody can derive a new trigger from a row's standing), the
// closing sentence branching on its presence, and a witness whose red is a superseded row rendered
// as though its trigger stood. The asymmetry is the design: supersession is authored because it
// carries content; "unchanged" is the ABSENCE of that claim and stays derived. Until then, an
// amendment that would move a trigger does not belong in this roster -- it belongs in the
// declaration, edited by the change that rules it, and this paragraph is what says so to the next
// lane rather than leaving them to splice prose into the projection again.

// WHY IT IS A SEPARATE CARRIER RATHER THAN A FIELD ON `RungDrop`. The precedent is inside
// gunbc.rung_drop itself: gunbc.deleted_cadence_reference_census carried `lost_with` BESIDE a drop
// rather than widening the shared record. The same two reasons hold here and one more. Most drops
// are never amended, so the field would be empty on 70-odd rows; the rows are one file each
// precisely so that separate lanes append without rewriting each other's file, and widening the
// record rewrites every one of them in a single diff; and an amendment is appended by a DIFFERENT
// change, at a different time, from the one that declared the drop -- which is the same argument
// DESIGN section 4b(3) makes for keeping the drop roster and the failure-mode ledger apart.
//
// THE JOIN IS THE RESIDUAL RISK AND IT IS WALLED. A row keyed by `drop_identity` can name a drop
// that does not exist, or one that was renamed. test.claim.rung_drop_amendment_witness_test joins
// every amendment against gunbc.rung_drop.roster at identity grain and refuses a dangling key, with
// a control amendment naming no drop to keep the assertion discriminating.

type RungDropAmendment {
  drop_identity: NonEmptyStr
  amended: String
  later_event: NonEmptyStr
  bearing: NonEmptyStr
}

// THE ONE RENDERING, for the same reason gunbc.rung_drop carries exactly one: a consumer that spelled
// the amendment itself would be a second authority for it and the two would drift.
fn rung_drop_amendment_message(a: RungDropAmendment, standing: RungDropStanding) -> String {
  concat(
    concat(concat("AMENDED ", a.amended), concat(": ", (a.later_event as String))),
    concat(
      concat("; ", (a.bearing as String)),
      concat(". ", standing_sentence(standing: standing))
    )
  )
}

// THE CLOSING SENTENCE, READ OFF THE ROW'S OWN STANDING. This is the derivation the note above
// argues for: the amendment supplies neither arm, so a row that is retired cannot be described by
// its amendment as one whose trigger still stands.
fn standing_sentence(standing: RungDropStanding) -> String {
  match standing {
    Standing => "The restoration trigger above stands whole."
    Retired { trigger_fired: _ } => "The trigger above has since fired; the retirement stated with this row is what ends the drop."
  }
}

// THE JOIN, AS A PREDICATE OVER BOTH POPULATIONS. Held here rather than in the roster so the
// witness can call it with a control amendment that names no drop.
fn every_amendment_names_a_rostered_drop(amendments: List<RungDropAmendment>, drop_identities: List<String>) -> Bool {
  fold(amendments, init: true, f: fn(acc, a) {
    acc && length(xs: filter(drop_identities, i => i == (a.drop_identity as String))) == 1
  })
}
